---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TreeLab from "../../components/TreeLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("tree");
const VL = "module/zfs/vdev_label.c";
const L = {
  rootbp: gh("include/sys/uberblock_impl.h", 128),
  metaDnode: gh("include/sys/dmu_objset.h", 81),
  blkptr: gh("include/sys/dnode.h", 257),
  nlevels: gh("include/sys/dnode.h", 216),
  indMax: gh("include/sys/dnode.h", 71),
  bpSize: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 361, 362),
  dsBp: gh("include/sys/dsl_dataset.h", 167),
  dirty: gh("module/zfs/dbuf.c", 2552),
  setdirty: gh("module/zfs/dbuf.c", 2583),
  newAddr: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 2102, 2104),
  rootSet: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 593),
  flush: gh(VL, 2085, 2098),
  even: gh(VL, 2109),
  pair: gh(VL, 1946, 1952),
  slot: gh(VL, 1782, 1784),
  ubWrite: gh(VL, 1792, 1796),
  odd: gh(VL, 2154),
  ubFlush: gh(VL, 1826, 1854),
  good: gh(VL, 1854),
  labels: gh("include/sys/vdev_impl.h", 562),
  boot: gh("include/sys/vdev_impl.h", 555, 560),
  ring: gh("include/sys/vdev_impl.h", 482),
  slotSize: gh("include/sys/vdev_impl.h", 491, 496),
  load: gh(VL, 1570, 1588),
  loadDone: gh(VL, 1547, 1553),
  labelCksum: gh(VL, 197, 200),
  readConfigCall: gh(VL, 1647),
  dirtyList: gh(VL, 1962, 1984),
  minVdevs: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 363),
  firstTxg: gh("module/zfs/spa.c", 4750, 4751),
  importUpdate: gh("module/zfs/spa.c", 5721, 5736),
  configUpdate: gh("module/zfs/spa_config.c", 489, 491),
  mmpTie: gh(VL, 1507, 1527),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="tree"
  title="The tree and the uberblock · Uberblok"
  description="How a ZFS pool changes state without fsck, read in the OpenZFS code: a tree of block pointers under one uberblock, a write that copies its path, and a commit in four beats that a power cut cannot tear: interactive, in 2D and 3D."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          ZFS has no fsck. After a crash, the pool must open either in the state before the last transaction group or in
          the state after it, never in a mixture, even though one txg writes thousands of blocks to several disks.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the whole change of state has to come down to one small write that either happened or did not. And that
          write must not be able to destroy the previous state if the power goes in the middle of it. The code gets there
          with a tree that hangs from a single pointer, a rule that a live block is never overwritten, and an order of
          writes at the end of every txg.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="tree">
      <h2 id="tree"><span class="n">1</span> Everything hangs from one pointer</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The uberblock holds one block pointer, <a href={L.rootbp}><code>ub_rootbp</code></a>. It points to the pool’s
          meta objset, the MOS, which begins with a dnode, <a href={L.metaDnode}>the meta-dnode</a>, whose blocks are blocks
          of dnodes. A dnode is the root of one object: its <a href={L.blkptr}><code>dn_blkptr[]</code></a> point to
          <a href={L.nlevels}><code>dn_nlevels</code></a> − 1 levels of indirect blocks, and the last level points to the
          data. Each pointer is a 128-byte <code>blkptr_t</code> that holds <a href={L.bpSize}>up to three addresses</a>
          and the checksum of the block below. From the uberblock down, nothing else is needed to find every block of the
          pool. The figure draws a file with three levels and four data blocks under the objset.
        </p>
      </div>
      <TreeLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="write">
      <h2 id="write"><span class="n">2</span> A write copies its whole path</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A changed block is never written in place: it gets <a href={L.newAddr}>a new address</a>. Its parent holds its
          pointer, address and checksum, so the parent changes too: <code>dbuf_dirty</code> <a href={L.dirty}>dirties the
          parent</a>, and so on up to the dnode, <a href={L.setdirty}>marked dirty</a> in its block of dnodes, up to the
          objset. One changed data block gives a new copy of itself and of every block on its path to the root; every
          other block is shared by the old tree and the new one. The old tree is untouched: the last synced uberblock still
          reaches all of it. In the figure, write D2, then D3 in the same txg: their common ancestors are copied once.
          The <a href={roomUrl(lang, "copy-on-write")}>next room</a> follows the checksums down that path.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="beats">
      <h2 id="beats"><span class="n">3</span> The commit, in four beats</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          At the end of a txg, once <code>spa_sync</code> has written the new blocks, <code>vdev_config_sync</code> runs
          four steps in a fixed order. First it <a href={L.flush}>flushes the write cache</a> of every disk written in the
          txg, so the new tree is on stable storage before anything points to it. Then it writes the <a href={L.even}>even
          labels</a>, L0 and L2, with the new configuration. Then the uberblock, into <a href={L.slot}>slot
          <code>ub_txg</code> mod the number of slots</a> of <a href={L.ubWrite}>all four labels</a>, which the uberblock of the previous txg
          does not occupy: that write is the commit, the moment the root pointer swings to the new tree. Last, the
          <a href={L.odd}>odd labels</a>, L1 and L3. Each disk carries <a href={L.labels}>four labels</a> of 256 KiB, two at
          its start and two at its end, and each label ends with a <a href={L.ring}>128 KiB ring</a> of uberblock slots: 128
          slots on the figure’s 512-byte sectors, <a href={L.slotSize}>32 on 4K-sector disks</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Labels are written only for <a href={L.dirtyList}>vdevs whose configuration changed</a> in this txg. In most txgs
          none did: beats 2 and 4 write nothing, and only the uberblock is new. Turn off “The vdev configuration changes in
          this txg” and step through again.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="plug">
      <h2 id="plug"><span class="n">4</span> Pull the plug, then import</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          On import, ZFS <a href={L.load}>reads every slot of every label</a>. A slot counts only if <a href={L.labelCksum}>its
          checksum</a> and its magic number are right, so a torn write is skipped; among the others, the
          <a href={L.loadDone}>highest <code>ub_txg</code> wins</a>. Then the configuration is read from <a href={L.readConfigCall}>the
          label with the newest config not newer than that uberblock</a>. Pull the plug at each step and import. Before beat
          3, no uberblock of the new txg exists: the old one wins, the even labels already written are newer than it and
          are passed over, the odd labels still match it. As soon as one copy of the new uberblock has landed, it wins, and
          the even labels match it. The tree is old or new, never mixed. Set how many uberblock writes land before a cut in
          beat 3: zero gives case (1) of the code’s comment, one or more gives case (2).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          Every block of the pool hangs from the uberblock’s one pointer, a write copies its path instead of overwriting it,
          and the new uberblock goes to a slot the old one does not hold, after the new tree is on disk: so the pool is
          either the old tree or the new one, whatever moment the power goes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>ub_rootbp</code> points to the MOS’s <code>objset_phys_t</code>; its meta-dnode’s blocks hold dnodes; a
          dnode’s <code>dn_blkptr[]</code> lead through <code>dn_nlevels</code> − 1 levels of indirect blocks to the data.
          <code>dbuf_dirty</code> dirties a block’s parent, recursively, so a changed leaf gives new copies of the leaf and
          all its ancestors at sync, each at a new address. <code>vdev_config_sync</code> then flushes the disks written in
          the txg, writes the config of the even labels, writes the uberblock into slot <code>ub_txg %
          VDEV_UBERBLOCK_COUNT</code> of all four labels and flushes it, and writes the odd labels. On import,
          <code>vdev_uberblock_load</code> keeps the valid uberblock with the highest txg, and
          <code>vdev_label_read_config</code> takes the label whose config txg is the highest not above it. A cut before any
          new uberblock lands leaves the old uberblock and the odd labels; a cut after one lands leaves the new uberblock and
          the even labels. Either pair is consistent.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p><strong>The top of the tree.</strong> The uberblock names a txg and one block pointer.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="include/sys/uberblock_impl.h"
          from={122}
          to={139}
          symbol="struct uberblock"
          notes={[
            { from: 125, text: "<code>ub_txg</code>: the txg this uberblock commits. On import, the highest valid one wins." },
            { from: 127, text: "<code>ub_timestamp</code>: the tie-breaker between two uberblocks of the same txg." },
            { from: 128, text: `<code>ub_rootbp</code>: the only pointer. <code>dsl_pool_sync_mos</code> <a href="${L.rootSet}">sets it</a> once the MOS is written.` },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="include/sys/dmu_objset.h"
          from={80}
          to={95}
          symbol="objset_phys_t"
          notes={[
            { from: 81, text: `An objset begins with a whole dnode, the meta-dnode. Its blocks are the objset’s blocks of dnodes: object <var>n</var> is dnode <var>n</var>. A filesystem’s own objset hangs from the MOS the same way, by its dataset’s <a href="${L.dsBp}"><code>ds_bp</code></a>.` },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="include/sys/dnode.h"
          from={213}
          to={229}
          symbol="dnode_phys_t"
          notes={[
            { from: 215, text: `The size of the object’s indirect blocks, <a href="${L.indMax}">at most 128 KiB</a>: 1024 block pointers of 128 bytes.` },
            { from: 216, text: "The height of the object’s tree; 1 when the dnode points straight at data." },
            { from: 217, text: `How many of the root pointers, <a href="${L.blkptr}"><code>dn_blkptr[]</code></a>, are used.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>A write climbs to the root.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/dbuf.c"
          from={2537}
          to={2554}
          symbol="dbuf_dirty"
          notes={[
            { from: 2537, text: "A block below the top level of its object has a parent indirect block." },
            { from: 2542, to: 2545, text: "The parent is at level + 1, block number <code>blkid >> epbs</code>, with 2<sup>epbs</sup> pointers per indirect block (1024 for 128 KiB). The figure uses 2, to stay small." },
            { from: 2552, text: `Dirtying a block dirties its parent, and so on up; the top level is held by the dnode, <a href="${L.setdirty}">marked dirty</a> in turn. Every dirty block is written to <a href="${L.newAddr}">a new place</a> at sync.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>The four beats.</strong> First the flush.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={VL}
          from={2085}
          to={2098}
          symbol="vdev_config_sync"
          notes={[
            { from: 2085, to: 2090, text: "The reason for the flush, in the code’s words: the blocks of this txg reach stable storage “before any uberblock that references them”." },
            { from: 2093, to: 2098, text: "Every vdev written in this txg, flushed; the wait covers them all." },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={VL}
          from={2100}
          to={2139}
          symbol="vdev_config_sync"
          notes={[
            { from: 2100, to: 2108, text: "Beat 2, and what a cut during it means: even labels newer than any uberblock are invalid, the odd ones still valid." },
            { from: 2109, text: `The even labels: <code>vdev_label_sync</code> writes labels <code>l</code> and <code>l + 2</code>, <a href="${L.pair}">L0 and L2</a>.` },
            { from: 2118, to: 2131, text: "Beat 3, and the two cases of a cut during it: no new uberblock landed, or at least one did. The figure replays both." },
            { from: 2133, text: `The uberblocks; <code>vdev_uberblock_sync_list</code> then <a href="${L.ubFlush}">flushes them</a>, and succeeds if <a href="${L.good}">at least one write</a> did.` },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={VL}
          from={1768}
          to={1797}
          symbol="vdev_uberblock_sync"
          notes={[
            { from: 1768, to: 1781, text: "Why the slot must differ from the current one: a torn overwrite of the committed uberblock could lose it. The comment is about RAID-Z expansion, which reuses a txg; the slot rule below serves every txg." },
            { from: 1782, to: 1784, text: "The slot: <code>ub_txg</code> modulo the slot count (one fewer with multihost). Consecutive txgs take consecutive slots; the next txg never lands on the slot of the previous one." },
            { from: 1792, to: 1796, text: "The same slot in all four labels of the disk." },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={VL}
          from={2144}
          to={2161}
          symbol="vdev_config_sync"
          notes={[
            { from: 2145, to: 2152, text: "Beat 4: the odd labels last. A cut here leaves the even labels and the new uberblocks, which suffice." },
            { from: 2154, text: "The same function as beat 2, with <code>l = 1</code>: L1 and L3." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Which disks get labels and uberblocks: <a href={L.dirtyList}>only the vdevs on the dirty list</a> get labels;
            with that list empty, the uberblock goes to <a href={L.minVdevs}>up to three</a> top-level vdevs.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/spa.c"
          from={10792}
          to={10819}
          symbol="spa_sync_rewrite_vdev_config"
          notes={[
            { from: 10792, to: 10815, text: "No configuration change: up to three top-level vdevs, from a random one, skipping logs and vdevs without metaslabs. A pool of one disk gets every uberblock on that disk." },
            { from: 10816, to: 10818, text: "A configuration change: every top-level vdev." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>Where the labels are.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={VL}
          from={158}
          to={170}
          symbol="vdev_label_offset"
          notes={[
            { from: 165, to: 166, text: "The offset falls inside one label, and the device size is a multiple of a label." },
            { from: 168, to: 169, text: `Labels 0 and 1 at the start, 2 and 3 in the last 512 KiB. On the figure’s 1 GiB disk: 0, 256 KiB, 1023.5 MiB and 1023.75 MiB. After the two front labels comes <a href="${L.boot}">a 3.5 MiB boot area</a>.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <strong>The import.</strong> Every slot of every label is read, and <a href={L.loadDone}>a valid one is kept if it
            beats the best so far</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={VL}
          from={1495}
          to={1505}
          symbol="vdev_uberblock_compare"
          notes={[
            { from: 1498, text: "The higher txg wins." },
            { from: 1503, text: `On the same txg, the later timestamp; then the <a href="${L.mmpTie}">multihost sequence</a>, which the figure leaves out.` },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={VL}
          from={832}
          to={863}
          symbol="vdev_label_read_config"
          notes={[
            { from: 835, to: 837, text: `A label counts only if its read succeeds, and the read checks <a href="${L.labelCksum}">the label’s own checksum</a>: a torn label is skipped.` },
            { from: 852, to: 856, text: `The label with the highest config txg not above <code>txg</code>, the winning uberblock’s (<a href="${L.readConfigCall}">the call</a>). This is how even labels of a txg that did not commit are passed over.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            After the import, the next txg is <a href={L.firstTxg}>the winner’s + 1</a>: when the old tree won, the txg that
            was lost is written again, and its uberblock goes to the same slot, over the torn one. The import also
            <a href={L.importUpdate}>asks for a config update</a>, which <a href={L.configUpdate}>marks every vdev dirty</a>
            for that txg: it rewrites the four labels, torn ones included, whatever else it changes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        One dataset and very few blocks: two pointers per indirect block instead of up to 1024, the meta-dnode’s own
        indirect levels and the step from the MOS to a filesystem’s objset folded into one block of dnodes. One disk, so
        every uberblock and every label lands on it; its 128 slots are those of 512-byte sectors. The writes of one beat are
        issued together in the code; the figure lands them in order (L0 then L2, L1 then L3, the uberblocks L0 to L3) and
        tears the one in flight when the plug is pulled. The content of the config, the retry with
        <code>ZIO_FLAG_IO_RETRY</code>, multihost, RAID-Z expansion, several sync passes in one txg and the freeing of the
        old blocks (the <a href={roomUrl(lang, "snapshots")}>snapshots room</a>) are not shown. The 3D view draws the same
        state as the 2D one.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="tree" />
</Base>

